def BACnetStack_SetSegmentationEnabled(
    deviceInstance,
    segmentationSupported,
    maxSegmentsAccepted,
    apduSegmentTimeout,
    proposedWindowSize,
    actualWindowSize,
    maxSegmentsTransmittable,
    enabled,
):
    """Enables segmentation and sets up the various parameters used by the segmentation logic.
    Also sets up the properties used by the device for segmentation

    RANGES BELOW ARE ENFORCED; an out-of-range value returns false and changes nothing.
    maxSegmentsAccepted must be 1 (0 is rejected). proposedWindowSize and actualWindowSize
    must
    each be 1..127 (MIN_WINDOW_SIZE..MAX_WINDOW_SIZE); 0 is rejected. apduSegmentTimeout
    must be
    non-zero (cl. 12.11.27; issue #1015) - 0 is rejected unconditionally, not only when
    Number_Of_APDU_Retries is non-zero, since no legitimate configuration needs a zero
    segment
    timeout. maxSegmentsTransmittable is still NOT validated - any value, including 0, is
    accepted.

    apduSegmentTimeout is in MILLISECONDS, as cl. 12.11.27 defines APDU_Segment_Timeout, and
    is
    advertised over the wire in those units. The spec-suggested default of 5000 means five
    seconds. (Before this was fixed the internal segment timer added the raw millisecond
    count
    to CallbackGetSystemTime(), which is in SECONDS, so 5000 produced a 5000-second timeout
    and
    segmentation effectively never timed out. The timer now converts. Because that clock has
    one-second resolution the conversion rounds UP with a one-second floor, so a sub-second
    timeout behaves as one second rather than as already-expired.)

    If you were compensating for the old behaviour by passing a small number - 5 to mean
    five
    seconds - that now means 5 ms, which the floor turns into one second. Pass 5000.

    The trailing `enabled` argument turns segmentation OFF when false:
    Segmentation_Supported
    becomes noSegmentation, Max_Segments_Accepted is reset to 1, and the
    Max_Segments_Accepted
    and APDU_Segment_Timeout optional properties are disabled. EVERY OTHER ARGUMENT IS
    IGNORED
    when enabled is false, and the call returns true without validating them

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetSegmentationEnabled(const uint32_t deviceInstance,
    const uint8_t segmentationSupported, const uint32_t maxSegmentsAccepted, const uint32_t
    apduSegmentTimeout, const uint8_t proposedWindowSize, const uint8_t actualWindowSize,
    const uint8_t maxSegmentsTransmittable, const bool enabled)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device to enable segmentation [const uint32_t
            deviceInstance]
        segmentationSupported: (in) The segmentation option that the device supports, 0 = SegmentedBoth, 1 =
            SegmentedTransmit, 2 = SegmentedReceive, 3 = NoSegmentation. ONLY 1
            (SegmentedTransmit) and 3 (NoSegmentation) are ACCEPTED. 0 and 2 are listed for
            completeness of the BACnetSegmentation enumeration but are REJECTED - this stack
            does not implement segmented receive - and the call returns false. [const
            uint8_t segmentationSupported]
        maxSegmentsAccepted: (in) If the Segmentation_Supported property of the Device object has a value of
            SEGMENTED_TRANSMIT or NO_SEGMENTATION, this property shall have a value of 1. If
            the Segmentation_Supported property of the Device object has a value of
            SEGMENTED_BOTH or SEGMENTED_RECEIVE, then the value of this property shall have
            a value greater than 1. [const uint32_t maxSegmentsAccepted]
        apduSegmentTimeout: (in) The amount of time in milliseconds between retransmission of an APDU
            segment. A suggested default value for this property is 5000 milliseconds. This
            value shall be non-zero if the Device object property called
            Number_Of_APDU_Retries is non-zero. In order to achieve reliable communication,
            it is recommended that the values of the APDU_Segment_Timeout properties of the
            Device objects of all intercommunicating devices should contain the same value.
            [const uint32_t apduSegmentTimeout]
        proposedWindowSize: (in) Indicates the maximum number of message segments it is prepared to transmit
            before it must receive a SegmentACK. The means of choosing the Proposed Window
            Size are a local matter, except that the value shall be in the range 1 to 127,
            inclusive. The Proposed Window Size shall be carried by the parameter of that
            name in each segment of the Confirmed-Request-PDU [const uint8_t
            proposedWindowSize]
        actualWindowSize: (in) The supported actual window size that this device uses [const uint8_t
            actualWindowSize]
        maxSegmentsTransmittable: (in) The maximum number of segments this device is capable of send (usually do
            to memory constraints) [const uint8_t maxSegmentsTransmittable]
        enabled: (in) Whether segmentation is enabled. When false the six configuration arguments
            above (segmentationSupported, maxSegmentsAccepted, apduSegmentTimeout,
            proposedWindowSize, actualWindowSize, maxSegmentsTransmittable) are ignored and
            not validated. deviceInstance is still used to locate the device. Recommended
            value: true. [const bool enabled]

    Returns:
        True on success; false if the device does not exist or segmentation support was
        not compiled (STACK_OPTION_SEGMENTATION).
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetSegmentationEnabled(
        deviceInstance,
        segmentationSupported,
        maxSegmentsAccepted,
        apduSegmentTimeout,
        proposedWindowSize,
        actualWindowSize,
        maxSegmentsTransmittable,
        enabled,
    )


def BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings(deviceInstance, maxNumberOfSubscriptions, maxSupportedCOVLifetime):
    """Sets the COV subscription-table capacity and the maximum supported COV lifetime for
    subscriptions received by this device.

    The lifetime is a single device-wide bound shared by SubscribeCOV, SubscribeCOVProperty
    and
    SubscribeCOVPropertyMultiple.

    ENFORCEMENT IS REJECT, NOT CLAMP. A subscription request asking for a lifetime longer
    than
    maxSupportedCOVLifetime is answered with Error(services, valueOutOfRange); it is not
    silently
    shortened to the maximum.

    THE DEVICE DEFAULT IS 28800 SECONDS (8 hours) - MAX_SUPPORTED_COV_LIFETIME in
    BACnetStackConstants.h, the minimum the BACnet spec requires a device to support. Every
    device
    starts at that value, so it is what the stack enforced before this setter was called.

    PASS 0 FOR "USE THE DEVICE DEFAULT". A caller migrating from the removed
    BACnetStack_SetMaxActiveCOVSubscriptions has no v5 lifetime to copy - that export never
    expressed one - so 0 is accepted as the sentinel and substitutes 28800, making the
    migration
    behaviour-preserving without the caller having to know the number. Passing 28800
    explicitly is
    identical. Issue #448.

    NOTE 0 previously meant the near OPPOSITE of "default": a zero bound rejected every
    request
    carrying a non-zero lifetime (they all exceed the maximum) while still admitting
    indefinite,
    lifetime-0 subscriptions. If you deliberately want that behaviour, it is no longer
    reachable
    through this export. maxNumberOfSubscriptions = 0 continues to mean NO LIMIT.

    #931: THIS FUNCTION IS NOT ATOMIC. It applies maxNumberOfSubscriptions first, then
    maxSupportedCOVLifetime; if the second application fails, the first has ALREADY taken
    effect and
    this still returns false - the caller cannot tell a total failure from a half-applied
    one. The two
    settings are independent, so prefer the single-purpose
    BACnetStack_SetMaxActiveCOVSubscriptions and
    BACnetStack_SetMaxSupportedCOVLifetime below, each of which is unambiguous about what it
    changed.
    This function is kept for compatibility with its existing partial-application behaviour.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const
    uint32_t maxNumberOfSubscriptions, const uint32_t maxSupportedCOVLifetime)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device to set the maximum supported COV lifetime.
            [const uint32_t deviceInstance]
        maxNumberOfSubscriptions: (in) The maximum number of active COV subscriptions to support. If set to zero,
            no limit. [const uint32_t maxNumberOfSubscriptions]
        maxSupportedCOVLifetime: (in) The maximum supported COV lifetime in seconds. Valid range is 0 ..
            MAX_UINT32 - 1; exactly MAX_UINT32 is rejected. Pass 28800 for the device
            default. See the note above before passing 0. [const uint32_t
            maxSupportedCOVLifetime]

    Returns:
        True if successful, false if failed.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings(
        deviceInstance,
        maxNumberOfSubscriptions,
        maxSupportedCOVLifetime,
    )


def BACnetStack_SetMaxActiveCOVSubscriptions(deviceInstance, maxNumberOfSubscriptions):
    """Sets ONLY the maximum number of active COV subscriptions (SubscribeCOV /
    SubscribeCOVProperty
    table capacity) that a server device can contain. The atomic, single-purpose half of
    BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings - #931.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetMaxActiveCOVSubscriptions(const uint32_t
    deviceInstance, const uint32_t maxNumberOfSubscriptions)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device to configure. [const uint32_t deviceInstance]
        maxNumberOfSubscriptions: (in) The maximum number of active COV subscriptions to support. If set to zero,
            no limit. [const uint32_t maxNumberOfSubscriptions]

    Returns:
        True if successful, false if failed.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetMaxActiveCOVSubscriptions(deviceInstance, maxNumberOfSubscriptions)


def BACnetStack_SetMaxSupportedCOVLifetime(deviceInstance, maxSupportedCOVLifetime):
    """Sets ONLY the maximum supported COV lifetime for subscriptions received by this device.
    The
    atomic, single-purpose half of BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings - #931. See that function's
    doc for
    the lifetime semantics (reject-not-clamp, the 28800-second default, and the 0-sentinel
    behaviour) -
    they are identical here.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetMaxSupportedCOVLifetime(const uint32_t
    deviceInstance, const uint32_t maxSupportedCOVLifetime)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device to configure. [const uint32_t deviceInstance]
        maxSupportedCOVLifetime: (in) The maximum supported COV lifetime in seconds. Valid range is 0 ..
            MAX_UINT32 - 1; exactly MAX_UINT32 is rejected. Pass 28800 for the device
            default, or 0 to substitute the same default. [const uint32_t
            maxSupportedCOVLifetime]

    Returns:
        True if successful, false if failed.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetMaxSupportedCOVLifetime(deviceInstance, maxSupportedCOVLifetime)


def BACnetStack_SetCOVMultipleSettings(deviceInstance, maxNumberOfSubscriptions):
    """Sets ONLY the SubscribeCOVPropertyMultiple subscription-table capacity. The parallel of
    BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings (which sets BOTH the single-COV and the COVPropertyMultiple
    tables to one value): call BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings first
    for a common cap, then this to override the COVPM table independently (e.g. cap the
    single-COV table
    small so a bounded request sequence exhausts it ->
    RESOURCES/NO_SPACE_TO_ADD_LIST_ELEMENT, while keeping
    the COVPM table large enough for multi-context tests).

    *Capacity only - there is deliberately no COVPropertyMultiple lifetime parameter here.**
    Despite the
    name symmetry with BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings(deviceInstance, maxSubscriptions,
    maxLifetime), this
    function takes two arguments, not three. The maximum supported COV lifetime is a single
    DEVICE-WIDE
    value (one m_maxSupportedCOVLifetime), shared by SubscribeCOV and
    SubscribeCOVPropertyMultiple alike;
    set it with BACnetStack_SetCOVSettings. A third parameter here would imply a per-table
    lifetime knob
    that the stack does not model.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetCOVMultipleSettings(const uint32_t deviceInstance,
    const uint32_t maxNumberOfSubscriptions)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device to configure. [const uint32_t deviceInstance]
        maxNumberOfSubscriptions: (in) Capacity of the SubscribeCOVPropertyMultiple table; 0 = unbounded. [const
            uint32_t maxNumberOfSubscriptions]

    Returns:
        True if successful, false if failed.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetCOVMultipleSettings(deviceInstance, maxNumberOfSubscriptions)


def BACnetStack_SetBackupAndRestoreEnabled(
    deviceInstance,
    backupPreparationTime,
    restorePreparationTime,
    restoreCompletionTime,
    backupFailureTimeout,
    enabled,
):
    """Enables or disables the Backup and Restore functionality on a device.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetBackupAndRestoreEnabled(const uint32_t
    deviceInstance, const uint16_t backupPreparationTime, const uint16_t
    restorePreparationTime, const uint16_t restoreCompletionTime, const uint16_t
    backupFailureTimeout, const bool enabled)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device to enable or disable the Backup and Restore
            services on. [const uint32_t deviceInstance]
        backupPreparationTime: (in) The time in seconds that the device might remain unresponsive at the start
            of a backup procedure. [const uint16_t backupPreparationTime]
        restorePreparationTime: (in) The time in seconds that the device might remain unresponsive at the start
            of a restore procedure. [const uint16_t restorePreparationTime]
        restoreCompletionTime: (in) The time in seconds that the device might remain unresponsive at the end of
            a restore procedure. [const uint16_t restoreCompletionTime]
        backupFailureTimeout: (in) The initial Backup_Failure_Timeout in seconds: how long the device waits
            before unilaterally ending a backup or restore procedure (135-2024 cl. 12.11.38,
            Unsigned16). The property stays writable over the wire afterwards; this is the
            power-on value. PASS A NON-ZERO VALUE. A zero timeout expires the failure timer
            on the next tick after STARTBACKUP, so the backup silently resets itself and
            never completes. [const uint16_t backupFailureTimeout]
        enabled: (in) True to enable the Backup and Restore properties, false to disable them.
            Disabling also empties the configuration-file list, since Configuration_Files
            itself is turned off. \\note This call and BACnetStack_AddFileObject(...,
            isConfigurationFile = true) may be made in EITHER ORDER. Enabling configures the
            feature; it does not discard configuration files that have already been
            declared. Enabling used to clear the list, so adding files first left
            Configuration_Files silently empty (issue #292). [const bool enabled]

    Returns:
        True if successful, false if failed.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetBackupAndRestoreEnabled(
        deviceInstance,
        backupPreparationTime,
        restorePreparationTime,
        restoreCompletionTime,
        backupFailureTimeout,
        enabled,
    )


