def BACnetStack_UpdateValue(deviceInstance, objectType, objectInstance, propertyIdentifier):
    """Tells the CAS BACnet Stack that a value has changed, and runs every piece of logic
    attached to that
    data point: COV and COV-property-multiple notifications, COV-driven trend logging, the
    deferred
    BUFFER_READY evaluation for any trend log that grew, and the intrinsic event and fault
    algorithms.

    REQUIRED. Every algorithm armed by the BACnetStack_SetIntrinsic* and
    BACnetStack_SetFault*
    functions is DORMANT until this function is called for the monitored property -
    typically
    Present_Value, but Total_Record_Count for BUFFER_READY, Member_Status_Flags for a
    globalGroup and
    Timer_State for a timer. A host that never calls it will never generate an event
    notification.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_UpdateValue(const uint32_t deviceInstance, const
    uint16_t objectType, const uint32_t objectInstance, const uint32_t propertyIdentifier)

    Args:
        deviceInstance: (in) The instance of the device that contains the object where the value
            changed. [const uint32_t deviceInstance]
        objectType: (in) The BACnet object type of the object where the value changed. See
            #CASBACnetStack::BACnetObjectType for more info. [const uint16_t objectType]
        objectInstance: (in) The instance of the object where the value changed. [const uint32_t
            objectInstance]
        propertyIdentifier: (in) The property of the value that changed. See #BACnetPropertyIdentifier for
            more info. [const uint32_t propertyIdentifier]

    Returns:
        True if the value update was processed. False if the device does not exist, if
        the property is not enabled on that object, or - TRAP - if the device advertises
        SubscribeCOV or SubscribeCOVProperty but the build did not define
        STACK_OPTION_SUBSCRIBE_COV, in which case this returns BEFORE the alarm and
        event evaluation runs at all. A second trap: for propertyIdentifier Object_Name
        or Object_Identifier the device's Database_Revision is incremented BEFORE the
        property-enabled check, so a call that returns false can still have bumped the
        revision.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_UpdateValue(
        deviceInstance,
        objectType,
        objectInstance,
        propertyIdentifier,
    )


# ---- Process Functions ----


def BACnetStack_DecodeAs(
    decodeType,
    inMessageRaw,
    inMessageLength,
    outMessage,
    outMessageMaxLength,
    packetNetworkType,
):
    """Decodes a raw BACnet message into a human-readable rendering of the packet, for
    debugging,
    logging, and client-side inspection of inbound messages.

    Replaces BACnetStack_DecodeAsXML() and BACnetStack_DecodeAsJSON(), which were the same
    function apart from the renderer they used. Pass the format as the first argument:

    BACnetStack_DecodeAs(BACnetDecodeType_JSON, raw, rawLen, out, sizeof(out),
    packetNetworkType);

    Sizing the output buffer: renderings are much larger than the wire message. A
    ReadPropertyMultiple response can exceed 20 KB; 64 KB is a safe general-purpose buffer.
    If the rendering does not fit, NOTHING is written and the function returns 0 - it does
    not
    truncate, and the return value does not tell you how much space would have been needed.

    Compile-time gating: XML requires STACK_OPTION_DECODE_AS_XML, JSON requires
    STACK_OPTION_DECODE_AS_JSON, and BOTH require STACK_OPTION_STRING_LITERALS. The two
    format
    options remain independent, so a build can include one renderer and not the other;
    asking
    for a format that was not compiled in returns 0 and logs.

    C declaration: uint32_t BACnetStack_DecodeAs(const uint8_t decodeType, const char*
    inMessageRaw, const uint16_t inMessageLength, char* outMessage, const uint32_t
    outMessageMaxLength, const uint8_t packetNetworkType)

    Args:
        decodeType: (in) The output format. See #BACnetDecodeType. [const uint8_t decodeType]
        inMessageRaw: (in) The raw BACnet message. [const char* inMessageRaw]
        inMessageLength: (in) The length of the BACnet message. [const uint16_t inMessageLength]
        outMessage: (out) The resulting rendered output. Not null-terminated; use the return value
            as the length. [char* outMessage]
        outMessageMaxLength: (in) The maximum buffer length for the rendered output. [const uint32_t
            outMessageMaxLength]
        packetNetworkType: (in) The BACnet network type the raw message came from (IP, MSTP, SC). This is
            the one deliberate exception to the Network Port instance convention every other
            Send/Receive export follows (#822/#556 Q2): DecodeAs is a pure decoder that can
            be handed bytes captured from a transport this device does not itself have a
            Network Port object for (e.g. a packet capture from another device), so it takes
            the 3-valued transport-type enum directly rather than resolving a claimed
            instance. [const uint8_t packetNetworkType]

    Returns:
        The number of bytes written to outMessage, or 0 on any failure (unknown
        decodeType, format not compiled in, invalid packetNetworkType, undecodable
        message, or buffer too small).
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_DecodeAs(
        decodeType,
        inMessageRaw,
        inMessageLength,
        outMessage,
        outMessageMaxLength,
        packetNetworkType,
    )


