def BACnetStack_SetBACnetSCWebSocketStatus(uri, uriLength, status, errorCode):
    """Reports the current status of a BACnet/SC WebSocket connection to the stack (e.g. after
    the host
    application's WebSocket layer connects, disconnects, or errors), so the stack's
    connection-state
    machine and Network Port status properties stay in sync with the real transport state.

    C declaration: bool BACnetStack_SetBACnetSCWebSocketStatus(const char* uri, const
    uint16_t uriLength, const uint8_t status, const uint32_t errorCode)

    Args:
        uri: (in) The WebSocket URI the status applies to. [const char* uri]
        uriLength: (in) The WebSocket URI the status applies to. [const uint16_t uriLength]
        status: (in) The BACnet/SC connection status being reported. See
            #CASBACnetStack::BACnetSCConstants::BACnetSCWebsocketStatus: Unknown = 0,
            Connecting = 1, Connected = 2, Disconnected = 3, Error = 4. [const uint8_t
            status]
        errorCode: (in) The error code associated with the status, if applicable (0 if none).
            [const uint32_t errorCode]

    Returns:
        True if a websocket or peer matching uri was found and updated, otherwise false
        (including when no BACnet/SC data link exists, or the stack was built without
        STACK_OPTION_DATA_LINK_LAYER_SC). \\note URI lengths are limited to
        BACnetSCConstants::BACNET_SC_MAX_URI_LENGTH (255); longer URIs are rejected.
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_SetBACnetSCWebSocketStatus(uri, uriLength, status, errorCode)


def BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackInitiateWebsocket(FPCallbackInitiateWebsocket):
    """Registers the callback the stack calls to ask the host to OPEN a WebSocket/TLS
    connection to a URI.
    The stack implements no WebSocket or TLS itself. Fires from inside BACnetStack_Tick(),
    from the
    BACnet/SC connection state machines.

    THE RETURN IS READ AT ONE SITE ONLY. The direct-connect initiator advances its state
    machine only
    when you return true; the hub connector marks the socket "connecting" BEFORE calling you
    and
    DISCARDS your return entirely. Either way the real answer must come back through
    BACnetStack_SetBACnetSCWebSocketStatus - returning true is a promise to attempt the
    connection,
    not a report that it succeeded. Do not block; open asynchronously and report status
    later.

    The URI is stack-owned and NUL-terminated, valid only for the duration of the call; the
    length
    excludes the terminator.

    Unregistered, the stack logs and simply never attempts a connection - there is no error
    and no
    retry ceiling. Registering has no effect if BACnet/SC was not compiled
    (STACK_OPTION_DATA_LINK_LAYER_SC).

    C declaration: void
    BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackInitiateWebsocket(bool(*FPCallbackInitiateWebsocket)(const
    char* websocketUri, const uint32_t websocketUriLength))

    Args:
        FPCallbackInitiateWebsocket: (in) The function pointer for the CallbackInitiateWebsocket function. See
            #CASBACnetStack::FPCallbackInitiateWebsocket for more info.
            [bool(*FPCallbackInitiateWebsocket)(const char* websocketUri, const uint32_t
            websocketUriLength)]
    """
    return _lib().BACnetStack_RegisterCallbackInitiateWebsocket(FPCallbackInitiateWebsocket)


